1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,210
Yet cold conditions can slow that loss.

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:08,940
And some DNA can bind to mineral surfaces in the
sediment, which may help protect it.

3
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:12,150
The dirt was not merely covering the clues.

4
00:00:12,150 --> 00:00:14,660
Parts of it may have helped them last.

5
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:22,220
But protection came with a catch: material that
holds on to DNA can also make it difficult to get
the DNA back out.

6
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:25,710
Researchers had to extract and read what remained.

7
00:00:25,710 --> 00:00:28,430
Not an untouched instruction book.

8
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:32,710
Fragments of one, mixed with fragments from many
other lives.

9
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:35,000
Enough to investigate, perhaps.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,320
But a fragment does not arrive wearing a name tag.

11
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,800
How do you know which animal you are reading?

12
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:45,780
Compare the surviving sequences with genetic
references.

13
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:47,050
Look for matches.

14
00:00:47,050 --> 00:00:52,680
That sounds simple, until two related animals share
similar stretches of DNA.

15
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:55,870
Or the reference you need is missing.

16
00:00:55,870 --> 00:01:00,230
A match can support a family branch without
identifying one exact species.

17
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:00,233
There is another trap: modern contamination could
resemble the signal you hoped to find.
